{{ template "agent-instructions.md" (dict "self" "claude" "root" .) }}
- Your built-in web search omits some public/community sources; reach for the
  Exa MCP for that research rather than treating the gap as the web's.
- Sandbox/permission questions (why a path/command/domain is blocked, how to allow
  it): read `~/.claude/docs/sandbox.md` before spawning a lookup agent.
- Model/tier & subscription-limit questions (pins, subagent tiers, cross-model
  routing to Codex, the codex bridge/plugin, the pi trial): read
  `~/.claude/docs/harness.md` before spawning a lookup agent.
- When a body of work is ready to hand back and it touches a high-stakes
  surface (auth or security boundaries, data loss or migration, concurrency, an
  external contract) or is broad enough to hide a defect (a cross-cutting
  change spanning roughly five or more files), run the `codex-review` skill
  once, unprompted: Codex proposes, you stay the implementer and adjudicate —
  substantiate each finding independently, fix only what survives, and say
  which you dropped and why. Once per body of work even when it spans
  sessions — not per session, not per edit; everything else belongs to
  self-review. Review yield tracks risk and breadth, not change count, and an
  unadjudicated cross-model reviewer can degrade stronger work — treat findings
  as hypotheses, never a verdict to apply.
- Before committing to an architecture or approach decision that is expensive
  to reverse, or when a bug resists a second diagnosis, get a decorrelated read
  via `codex-advisor` — unprompted, and before presenting a plan for approval,
  not after; reversal cost is highest before implementation.
- At task intake, if the work already looks top-model-grade (hardest long-horizon
  architecture/synthesis), say so before building up context so I can restart it
  in a fresh top-model session; never suggest `/model`-bumping a grown session —
  the bump re-meters the whole grown context at the higher rate every turn.
